WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:05.129
Today's video is brought to you by InnovativeLanguage, the creators of FinnishPod101.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.830
Click the link in the description to check out their huge collection of audio and video lessons for students of all levels.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.860
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel, and my name is Paul.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.620
Today, we're going to compare two languages that are closely related to each other and similar in many ways,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.459
but also quite different from each other, with limited mutual intelligibility.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.910
I'm talking about two non-Indo-European languages of northern Europe: Finnish and Estonian.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:40.260
Finnish and Estonian are both members of the Finnic group of languages, along with Karelian, Ludic, Veps,

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.079
Ingrian, Votic, and Livonian.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.780
Their most recent common ancestor language was Late Proto Finnic, around 2,000 years ago.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:56.489
Finnic is a branch of the Uralic language family. So, they are both more distantly related to Hungarian, as well.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.279
Since Finnish and Estonian have been developing in different directions for 2000 years,

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:07.709
they are now fairly different, with Estonian being much more innovative, while Finnish is more conservative.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:15.570
In other words, Estonian has changed more. They're not mutually intelligible—for the most part—aside from basic general vocabulary.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.089
Estonian speakers generally understand Finnish better than Finnish speakers understand

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:28.379
Estonian because of exposure to Finnish media. Though I understand that this is much less the case among the young generations today.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:34.019
Let's look at a few common phrases in either language and see what's similar and what's different about them.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:40.380
"Hello" in Finnish: "Hei", and in Estonian: "Tere" or "Tervist."

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.930
This word literally means "health."

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:45.629
The "t" here is a partitive case inflection.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.570
Partitive is the case used for direct objects when they are partial objects, rather than complete ones.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.290
"Tere" is a contraction of "Tervist,"

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:01.529
but Finnish also has the formal word "Terve" for "Hello," which literally means "healthy."

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:08.160
This is a cognate with the Estonian word "tervist," except "terve" is an adjective, rather than a noun.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:15.389
In Estonian, "Tere" is used with many greetings, while in Finnish, "Hyvää" is used.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.000
Estonian has a cognate of this word, "hea." They both mean "good."

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.560
Good morning in Finnish: "Hyvää huomenta."

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.180
This noun is in the partitive case and is a literary word from "morning" in Finnish.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:36.900
In Estonian: "Tere hommikust." Here you can see that these two words are cognates, and both come from Proto Finnic: "hoomen,"

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.780
but the Estonian word is in the elative case, which is used with the sense of "from" or "out of."

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:44.400
"Good day"

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:51.150
or "good afternoon" in Finnish: "Hyvää päivää," and in Estonian: "Tere päevast." Again, here

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:57.060
you can see that these two words are cognates. These cognates reveal an important difference between Finnish and Estonian.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.399
Finnish uses vowel harmony while Estonian no longer uses vowel harmony,

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.519
except in some archaic expressions. In Finnish,

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:10.319
there are three classes of vowels: front vowels, neutral vowels, and back vowels.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:19.760
If a word contains front vowels, it cannot contain back vowels, and vice versa. This also applies to the word suffixes: "päivää."

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.100
This is a front vowel. So, aside from this neutral vowel, the other vowel is also a front vowel.

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:31.529
"päevast": here, we see a front vowel and a neutral vowel, but there's a back vowel in the suffix.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.310
This word doesn't exhibit the same vowel harmony as the Finnish word.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.290
But, it's worth noting that vowel harmony may be seen in southern Estonian dialects.

00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:45.959
"Good evening" in Finnish: "Hyvää iltaa", and in Estonian: "Tere õhtust."

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.979
In this case, these phrases contain no cognate words.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.000
"What's your name?" in Finnish: "Mikä sinun nimesi on?" Word-for-word: "What your name is?"

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:02.039
"Sinun" is the genitive inflection of the personal pronoun "sinä."

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.809
"Sinun" also has a short form that is used in informal speech: "sun":

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:09.370
"Mikä sun nimes on?"

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:12.020
In Estonian: "Mis sinu nimi on?"

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.700
Just like in Finnish, Estonian also has a short form of the genitive pronoun:

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.280
"Mis su nimi on?" Whether we use the long forms or the short forms,

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.130
you can see that the Finnish and Estonian sentences are pretty close to each other. One difference is the word for "what."

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.509
Another difference is the form of the word for "name." In Finnish,

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:34.229
the basic nominative form is "nimi," like in Estonian.

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:41.249
But in Finnish, there are special possessive forms of the nominative. When there's a genitive pronoun to show possession, a

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:48.810
corresponding possessive suffix is added to the end of the noun. And in this case, it changes the final vowel, though that doesn't always happen.

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:56.939
The full suffix, "~si," is used in formal written Finnish, while a shortened suffix is usually used in spoken Finnish. Or, the

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:03.089
suffix is dropped: "Mikä sun nimi on?" When the suffix is dropped, it's more like the Estonian sentence.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.319
Possessive suffixes disappeared from Estonian in the 17th century.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:13.619
So, whereas in Finnish, you can say a possessive sentence without the genitive personal pronoun, in Estonian,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.540
you need it. For example, in the sentences meaning "My name is ______." in Finnish: "Nimeni on ______."

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.620
Here, there's no genitive pronoun, but there's a possessive suffix.

00:05:23.259 --> 00:05:28.799
In Estonian: "Minu nimi on _____." Here, there's a genitive pronoun, but there's no possessive suffix.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:33.329
False friends: so far we've seen that some words are different

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:39.000
and other words are cognates, while those cognates often have different forms or different inflectional forms.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:46.619
But, as with most languages that are closely related to each other, there are also some false friends- cognates that look and sound similar

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.370
but actually have different meanings. For example, in Finnish:

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.039
"koristaa" means "to decorate."

00:05:53.289 --> 00:05:59.489
The Estonian word "koristama" means "to clean." The core meaning of these words is "to make beautiful,"

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.959
so you can see how that developed into both of their present meanings. In Finnish,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:10.949
"maja" means a "shelter", or a "tree house". And in Estonian, "maja" is the regular word for "house."

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.719
The Finnish word "huone" means a "room" the Estonian word

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:19.139
"hoone" means a big building. The Finnish word

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:24.599
"hytii" means a "cabin," but a cabin on a boat. The Estonian word "hütt"

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.330
means a "hut", or a "shack."

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:33.419
Both of these words are Germanic loan words, the Finnish one from Swedish, and the Estonian one from Old High German.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.510
The Finnish word "raamattu" means "Bible." The Estonian word "raamat"

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:47.849
means "book." The word for "book" in Finnish is "kirja," and the word for "Bible" in Estonian is "Piibel."

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.390
Finnish is much more conservative in its vocabulary,

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:57.029
meaning that there have been strong efforts to use Finnish words or coin new words from Finnish roots

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:02.700
instead of adopting loan words for modern objects and concepts. For example, in Estonian,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:09.420
the word for "telephone" is "telefon." Finnish had the word "telefoni," but replaced it with for "puhelin."

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:11.880
Estonian contains many loan words,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.369
especially from Middle Low German and German.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:20.430
Finnish sticks to its Finnish roots for most of these. The word for "kitchen" in Finnish "keittiö."

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:27.180
And in Estonian: "köök." The Finnish word was coined in the 19th century from the word meaning "to cook,"

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:32.669
plus a suffix that forms nouns from verbs. The Estonian word comes from Middle Low German.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:39.629
Where Finnish does use loan words, they're often from Swedish, as we saw in a previous pair of false friends.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.230
Another example is the words for "doctor." In Finnish:

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:49.770
"lääkäri," which comes from Swedish. And in Estonian "arst," which comes from Middle Low German.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:51.850
Grammar:

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:58.140
Finnish and Estonian have many grammatical features in common. Both Finnish and Estonian are synthetic languages.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.860
That means that meaning can be added to a sentence by adding additional

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.840
morphemes to a word, rather than adding additional words. Both have a combination of

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:13.889
agglutinating and fusional elements, though Estonian has become somewhat less synthetic with the loss of some inflectional affixes,

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.979
like the loss of the possessive suffixes we saw before.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.880
Another example is that Estonian verbs—in their negative

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:27.029
conjugation—have no affixes showing person, making subject pronouns necessary, unlike in Finnish.

00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:33.288
Neither language has grammatical gender. There are no masculine-feminine-neuter distinctions.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.610
Neither language has articles, like "the" or "a" in English.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:44.060
For example, in Finnish "talo" means either "the house" or "a house." And also in Estonian: "maja"

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:46.458
means either "the house" or "a house."

00:08:47.399 --> 00:08:51.109
Both Finnish and Estonian have both prepositions and postpositions.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:57.199
Neither language has a separate future tense. The simple present is also used for the future.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:05.060
For example, these sentences can mean either "I study English" or "I will study English." In Finnish: "Opiskelen englantia."

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.880
And in Estonian: "Ma õpin inglise keelt."

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.700
But the Estonian sentence actually needs some more

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:17.959
context in order to be interpreted as future tense. If you add an adverb of time, like "tomorrow," then we'll know it's future tense,

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:23.299
rather than present tense:  “Homme õpin inglise keelt.” Without that context, the word

00:09:23.579 --> 00:09:26.059
"hakkan" could be used to indicate the future.

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:32.629
It literally means "start," but is used like "going to" in English: “Ma hakkan inglise keelt õppima."

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:41.249
Finnish and Estonian have similar case systems. Finnish has 15 cases. while Estonian has 14. Though

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.679
there are two others that are sometimes included, as well.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.829
There's no accusative case on the Estonian list,

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.870
but as in Finnish, the accusative form is the same as either the nominative or the genitive form.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.730
Grammatical differences:

00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:02.699
The inflections are different between the languages, of course. We saw some examples of that before in verb conjugations and in case inflections.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:09.989
Estonian syntax has some influence from Low German and German. For example, Estonian often uses postpositions,

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.740
whereas in Finnish, a case would be used.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:16.880
For example, the phrase meaning "on the table" in Finnish: "Pöydalla."

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.340
and in Estonian: "laua peal."

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:24.860
The Finnish noun is in the addessive case, which gives the meaning of "on" or "at." In Estonian,

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.989
there's a separate word, a postposition, meaning "on."

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:34.560
In Finnish, subject pronouns are normally left out for the first and second person, except for emphasis,

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:42.680
but the third person is required; that's in the written formal language. In casual speech, the pronouns are normally used, though not always.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:49.880
In Estonian, they're more often left out in casual speech. For example, these sentences mean "I read (past tense) a book." First, in formal

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:57.580
Finnish: "(Minä) luin kirjan" and in informal Finnish: "Mä luin kirjan." And in Estonian, with the pronoun:

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:04.060
"Ma lugesin raamatu läbi.” And without the pronoun: “Lugesin raamatu läbi.” While in Finnish the use of the pronoun

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.540
depends more on the register—with the pronoun

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:14.069
normally dropped in formal/written Finnish but included in spoken Finnish—in Estonian, the use of the pronoun doesn't really depend on the register.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:20.879
It's normal to either use or drop the pronoun in both formal/written and spoken Estonian. One other difference here

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:27.870
is that an Estonian, the word "läbi" is necessary because the object here is a whole object, like the accusative case,

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:31.620
it adds the meaning of "through," like "I read through the whole book."

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:37.440
This word would be unnecessary if the noun was in the partitive case, referring to only part of the book.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:44.340
Next, we're going to simply dive into some sentences and see what we find. These sentences mean:

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:50.000
"I study literature in university." In Finnish: "Mä opiskelen kirjallisuutta yliopistos."

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:58.320
"I | I study | literature | in university." And in Estonian: "Õpin ülikoolis kirjandust." Word for word,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.960
it's: "I study | in university | literature."

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:06.540
First, notice that the Finnish sentence contains a subject pronoun, while the Estonian sentence doesn't.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.320
Remember that in either language,

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:15.119
people can use or drop the pronoun, but in speech it's normally used in Finnish, while it's more often dropped in spoken Estonian.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.460
Next, we see a pair of cognates, meaning "study": "opiskelen" (Finnish), "Õpin" (Estonian)

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:25.440
There's an interesting difference here. The Finnish verb has a suffix that makes it frequentative,

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:31.200
meaning it shows a repeated action. It changes the meaning of the verb from "learn" to "study."

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:35.789
But if we remove the suffix, it's a lot more like the Estonian word: "opin"

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.459
The Estonian word can be used as it is, with the meaning of "study," even though it also means "learn."

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.250
Next, the words for "literature" are cognates.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:49.889
Both are in the partitive case, the case used for partial direct objects. In this case,

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:56.910
the person doesn't study literature and all its entirety. That would be impossible. The person studies only part of the literature available,

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:04.230
so the partitive case is used. But, obviously, even though the same case is used in both languages, the inflectional forms are rather different.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:07.440
Next, the words for "university" are different, though

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.469
both are in the inessive case, which adds the meaning of "in."

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:15.150
Note that the inflection of the Finnish word is an abbreviated colloquial form.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.500
It matches the informality of the sentence, shown by the short pronoun.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:24.300
And, one more sentence: "If you drove more slowly, I would be able to read the map."

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:28.980
In Finnish: "Jos ajaisit hitaammin, niin pystyisin lukemaan karttaa."

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.760
Word-for-word, it's:

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:36.700
"If | you would drive | more slowly | then | I would be able to | read | map." And in Estonian:

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:40.980
"Kui sa aeglasemalt sõidaksid, saaksin kaarti lugeda." Word-for-word, it's:

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:46.280
"If | you | more slowly | you would drive | I would be able to | map | read."

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.120
First, we see different words meaning "if."

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:55.379
Here, Finnish has no pronoun. Which tells us this is likely more formal Finnish. In Estonian,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:14:00.209
the pronoun is present, which is common in both formal and informal registers. Next,

00:14:00.209 --> 00:14:05.069
we see two different verbs meaning drive, but both are in the second person singular conditional.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.320
I'm sure you notice the difference in word order.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:11.759
This is interesting because in Estonian, the verb comes at the end of the if-clause.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:17.489
This likely shows the influence of Low German or Old High German. In German and Low German,

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:22.520
the conjugated verb comes at the end of subordinate clauses, of which if-clauses are one type.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:28.620
If we skip ahead to the second part of this—the main clause—we also see word order that looks Germanic.

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:33.359
The auxiliary verb is at the beginning, and the lexical verb comes at the end of the clause.

00:14:33.459 --> 00:14:39.839
The auxiliary verb is not in second position, as in German or Low German, because the subject pronoun is dropped here.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.180
But, we did see that word order before in this sentence: “Ma hakkan inglise keelt õppima.”

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:51.940
Here, the conjugated verb is in the second position, and the second verb comes at the end, as in German or Low German.

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:59.550
Next, we see two different comparative adverbs meaning "more slowly." In Finnish, adverbs are formed by adding

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:07.000
"sti" to the inflecting form of the adjective. Then, to form the comparative adverb, "sti" is replaced by "mmin."

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.480
In Estonian, the stem of adverbs is the genitive singular form of the adjective. To that,

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:18.420
we add the suffix "lt" (LT). Then to make the adverb comparative, you remove that ending and add "malt."

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:24.810
Next, in the finished sentence, we see "niin," meaning "then," to show the outcome of a condition.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:30.839
But in Estonian, it's not used. There is a word that can be used "siis," which serves the same function,

00:15:31.149 --> 00:15:33.599
but the sentence is fine without it. In Finnish,

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.200
It's better to include the word "niin," and the sentence would sound a bit strange without it.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.820
Next, we see two different verbs meaning "I would be able to." Both are in the first person singular conditional mood.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:51.820
Next, we have two cognate verbs meaning "to read," but these two verbs reveal a grammatical difference between Finnish and Estonian.

00:15:52.319 --> 00:15:59.399
The Finnish verb is a third active infinitive in the elated case. Finnish has 5 different infinitive forms

00:15:59.399 --> 00:16:01.739
And this one is used in verb chains like this.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:07.620
Estonian has only two types of infinitive: the "ma" infinitive and the "da" infinitive.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:14.069
This one is the "da" infinitive. The "da" infinitive is used when something may or may not actually occur,

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:19.980
whereas the "ma" infinitive is used for things that are certain. The "da" infinitive is used in sentences

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:27.260
meaning things like "she can," or "he may," or "we want," etc., because the action is not certain.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:33.760
And after that, we have cognate words for "map," both in the partitive case, but with different inflectional forms.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:36.980
As you can see, Finnish and Estonian

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:44.280
function very similarly, and their shared roots are obvious, but it's also obvious that they split off from each other a very long time ago.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:50.940
Estonian is, by far, the more innovative language and readily open to changes, while Finnish is much more conservative,

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:58.469
at least standard Finnish. If we introduced Finnish dialects into the comparison, there might be more similarities with Estonian.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:05.999
Standard Estonian was based more on Northern Estonian dialects as they were spoken, whereas standard Finnish was based more on

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:13.530
conservative forms that were common to all, or most, Finnish dialects. The question of the day: For speakers of Finnish and Estonian:

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:21.080
How well do you understand the other language? Do you think exposure to the other language would be enough to get used to it and understand it?

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.880
For other people: What's your impression of these two languages?

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.540
How similar do they seem to you?

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.260
If you're interested in learning Finnish,

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:31.619
I recommend you click the link in the description to check out FinnishPod101.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:36.989
They have hundreds of audio and video lessons with full transcripts, for students of all levels.

